//
// Probe: `resolve`, `resolve.class`, `do`, `do.`, `json.from_value` and
// `pull.workbench.`.
//
// **`resolve` runs what it finds; `resolve.class` pushes it.** The reference
// applies a PTR to the resolved name, so `resolve` *calls* the word rather
// than handing it back, and an alias is followed first. The class form is the
// odd one out in the pair.
//
// **`do` runs its lambda until the current stack is empty**, which is why the
// cases below use their own stacks: on `@main` a `{ drop }` body would eat
// everything the probe had accumulated. The guard is on entry only, so a
// lambda pulled off an otherwise empty stack still runs once.
//
// **`json.from_value` refuses more than it accepts.** INTEGER, FLOAT, BOOL,
// LIST and MAP convert, recursively; a STRING does not, and neither does
// NODATA — dt 97 is not NONE's dt 0, so the `null` arm does not reach it.
// Only the accepted set is here: a refusal renders through the diagnostic
// table, which F66 records as text no second machine reproduces.
//
// **`pull.workbench.` takes its name list off the workbench** and then drains
// what is left of it, where `pull.workbench` takes the list off the stack.
// Member order is F15's, which the capture normalises.
//
"probe: resolve follows an alias and runs the word it finds" println
:sq { dup * } register
5 "sq" resolve
"sq" "squared" alias
6 "squared" resolve

"probe: resolve.class pushes the class rather than running it" println
"Bool" resolve.class

"probe: json.from_value over the set it accepts" println
42 json.from_value
2.5 json.from_value
true json.from_value
[ 1 2 ] json.from_value
dict :a 1 set json.from_value

"probe: do runs a lambda until its stack is empty, on a stack of its own" println
@doer 1 2 3 { drop } do
@main

"probe: do. takes the lambda off the workbench and drains the stack" println
@doertwo 4 5 6
{ drop } { true } ?.
do.
@main

"probe: pull.workbench. names workbench values, list and all from there" println
@puller
11 { true } ?.
22 { true } ?.
[ "x" "y" ] { true } ?.
pull.workbench.
@main
